; expect 42
; A signature's type variables are the CONTROL for the rigidity rule
; in `tyCompat`: a rule written too wide refuses every one of these,
; and until 2026-08-10 a rule written too narrow (a variable matched
; anything at any depth) made writing `a` strictly weaker than writing
; `Int`. The refusals are in
; `tests/diagnostics/460-signature-type-variable.ax`; these are the
; programs that have to keep working.
;
;   idf     the identity. Its body answers the parameter, so the
;           declared result and the inferred one are the SAME
;           variable - which is the only pair rigidity accepts;
;   konst   two distinct variables in one signature, and a body that
;           answers the first. `(-> a b a)` says something now, and
;           the term that proves it is 460's `(fn (promised x y) y)`;
;   applyf  a variable under an ARROW, applied to a variable beside
;           it. The parameter `f` has type `(-> a b)` and `x` has type
;           `a`, so `(f x)` type-checks only if two occurrences of `a`
;           written in different places are recognised as one;
;   the calls  instantiate. Each reference mints fresh placeholders,
;           so a caller may still pass whatever it likes - `idf` takes
;           an Int here and the identity is not restricted to Int.
(:: idf (-> a a))
(fn (idf x) x)

(:: konst (-> a b a))
(fn (konst x y) x)

(:: applyf (-> (-> a b) a b))
(fn (applyf f x) (f x))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (if (== (+ (idf 20) (konst 21 99)) 41)
      (applyf (lambda (n) 42) 7)
      1))
